From: adb-rt (armv7-pc-windows-msvc port)
Subject: [PATCH] sysdeps_win32: flush stdio before _exit() in wmain()

adb's Windows entry point ends with

    _exit(main(argc, narrow_args.data()));

_exit() terminates the process without flushing the C runtime's stdio buffers.

When stdout is a CONSOLE it is unbuffered, so every write has already reached the
console and nothing is lost -- which is why this has never been noticed. When stdout
is a PIPE or a REDIRECT the CRT makes it FULLY BUFFERED (4 KB), and `adb devices -l`
emits far less than one buffer, so the entire output is discarded at exit and the
process still exits 0.

Measured, x64 twin of adb's output path (BATCH-ADB-7 section 3), stdout redirected:

    printf/fprintf/fputs/fwrite all return their byte counts (52/23/13/14)
    fflush(stdout) returns 0, errno 0
    the file is 0 bytes

    with setvbuf(stdout, NULL, _IONBF, 0) first  -> all 107 bytes land
    with an explicit fflush(stdout) before exit  -> all 107 bytes land

So the file descriptor is healthy and the data is buffered correctly; only the
exit-time flush is missing. stderr is unaffected because it is unbuffered.

This broke every consumer that reads adb's stdout rather than watching a console.
scrcpy was the first: it runs `adb devices -l` through a pipe, finds no
"List of devices attached" header, and sc_adb_parse_devices() returns false.

The one-line fix keeps the reason the original code used _exit() in the first place --
the comment above it asks NOT to destruct NarrowArgs, and _exit() still skips all
destructors and atexit handlers. It only adds the flush that the buffers need.

fflush(nullptr) flushes every open output stream, which is what the CRT's own exit
path would have done.

--- a/sysdeps_win32.cpp
+++ b/sysdeps_win32.cpp
@@ -2911,7 +2911,14 @@ extern "C" int main(int argc, char** argv);
 extern "C" int wmain(int argc, wchar_t **argv) {
     // Convert args from UTF-16 to UTF-8 and pass that to main().
     NarrowArgs narrow_args(argc, argv);

+    const int rc = main(argc, narrow_args.data());
+
+    // _exit() below does not flush stdio. On a console stdout is unbuffered so nothing is
+    // lost, but when stdout is a pipe or a redirect it is fully buffered and the whole
+    // output would be discarded -- silently, with exit code 0.
+    fflush(nullptr);
+
     // Avoid destructing NarrowArgs: argv might have been mutated to point to string literals.
-    _exit(main(argc, narrow_args.data()));
+    _exit(rc);
 }
